 in Lidded Jars</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#6-adjust-the-glass-shelves">6. Adjust the Glass Shelves</a></li></ul></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#use-the-door-and-every-inch">Use the Door and Every Inch</a><ul class="kb-table-of-contents-list-sub"><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#7-add-a-magnetic-strip-inside-the-door">7. Add a Magnetic Strip Inside the Door</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#8-stick-a-small-cup-on-the-door">8. Stick a Small Cup on the Door</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#9-label-the-shelves">9. Label the Shelves</a></li></ul></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#where-to-keep-medicines-instead">Where to Keep Medicines Instead</a><ul class="kb-table-of-contents-list-sub"><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#10-move-pills-to-a-cool-dry-drawer">10. Move Pills to a Cool, Dry Drawer</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#11-use-a-cabinet-with-a-child-latch">11. Use a Cabinet With a Child Latch</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#12-give-the-first-aid-kit-one-clear-spot">12. Give the First Aid Kit One Clear Spot</a></li></ul></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#ideas-for-small-bathrooms">Ideas for Small Bathrooms</a><ul class="kb-table-of-contents-list-sub"><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#13-add-a-surfacemounted-cabinet">13. Add a Surface-Mounted Cabinet</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#14-split-storage-between-cabinet-sink-and-toilet">14. Split Storage Between Cabinet, Sink and Toilet</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#15-do-a-fiveminute-monthly-reset">15. Do a Five-Minute Monthly Reset</a></li></ul></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#how-to-get-rid-of-old-medicines">How to Get Rid of Old Medicines</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#medicine-cabinet-mistakes-to-avoid">Medicine Cabinet Mistakes to Avoid</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#frequently-asked-questions">Frequently Asked Questions</a><ul class="kb-table-of-contents-list-sub"><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#what-should-be-kept-in-a-medicine-cabinet">What should be kept in a medicine cabinet?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#is-it-bad-to-keep-medicine-in-the-bathroom">Is it bad to keep medicine in the bathroom?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#how-do-you-organize-a-small-medicine-cabinet">How do you organize a small medicine cabinet?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#how-do-you-get-rid-of-expired-medicine">How do you get rid of expired medicine?</a></li></ul></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#final-thoughts">Final Thoughts</a></li></ul></div></nav>


<h2 class="wp-block-heading">What Belongs in a Medicine Cabinet?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Despite the name, the bathroom cabinet is not the best home for most medicines. MedlinePlus, from the U.S. National Library of Medicine, explains in its guide to <a href="https://medlineplus.gov/ency/patientinstructions/000534.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">storing your medicines</a> that heat and moisture from the shower, bath and sink may damage them. It recommends a cool, dry place instead, such as a dresser drawer or a kitchen cabinet away from the stove, sink and hot appliances, and always out of reach and out of sight of children.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Good medicine cabinet organization starts by freeing the cabinet for what you actually use at the sink: toothpaste, floss, skincare, deodorant, cotton rounds and small grooming tools. The map below shows where each group goes.</p>



