<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Quick Pick: Medicine Cabinet Organization by Goal</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Your goal</th><th>Best ideas</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Find things fast</td><td>#1, #2, #3, #5</td><td>$0–20</td></tr><tr><td>More space in a shallow cabinet</td><td>#6, #7, #8</td><td>$0–25</td></tr><tr><td>Store medicines safely</td><td>#10, #11, #12</td><td>$0–25</td></tr><tr><td>Small bathroom, little storage</td><td>#13, #14</td><td>$40+</td></tr><tr><td>Keep it tidy</td><td>#4, #9, #15</td><td>$0–10</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Organize a Medicine Cabinet in 7 Steps</h2>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Empty everything.</strong> Take every item out and lay it on a towel on the counter.</li>



<li><strong>Check dates.</strong> Set aside expired products and anything that has changed color, texture or smell.</li>



<li><strong>Dispose of old medicines safely.</strong> Use a take-back option where possible (see below).</li>



<li><strong>Sort what is left.</strong> Make groups: dental, skin, hair, first aid, tools and medicines.</li>



<li><strong>Move medicines out.</strong> Put pills and other medicines in a lidded box for a cool, dry spot.</li>



<li><strong>Wipe and set the shelves.</strong> Clean the glass, then adjust shelf heights to fit your tallest bottles.</li>



<li><strong>Put things back by use.</strong> Daily items at eye level, backups up high, tools on the door.</li>
</ol>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Medicine Cabinet Organization Ideas for Every Shelf</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">1. Organize Shelves by How Often You Use Them</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This one rule does most of the work. Put the things you reach for every morning, like toothpaste, deodorant and face cream, on the eye-level shelf. Backups and once-a-week items go on the top shelf, and taller bottles sit on the bottom shelf where there is more height.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-03-shelves-by-use.jpg" alt="Medicine cabinet with daily items like toothpaste, deodorant, face cream and toothbrushes on the middle shelf and less-used items in a lidded box on the top shelf" class="wp-image-1663" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-03-shelves-by-use.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-03-shelves-by-use-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-03-shelves-by-use-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-03-shelves-by-use-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">2. Group Small Things in Clear Bins</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Hair ties, cotton swabs and travel tubes roll around and fall out when you open the door. Narrow clear bins keep each group together so you can see what you have at a glance. Measure the inside depth of your cabinet before you buy, since many are shallow. The same trick works in a <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/junk-drawer-organizer/">junk drawer organizer</a>.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-02-clear-bins.jpg" alt="Three clear acrylic bins on a glass medicine cabinet shelf holding hair ties, cotton swabs and small tubes, sorted by type" class="wp-image-1662" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-02-clear-bins.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-02-clear-bins-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-02-clear-bins-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-02-clear-bins-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">3. Use a Small Turntable in a Deeper Cabinet</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If your cabinet is deep enough (measure first; a vanity cabinet usually is, and some surface-mounted ones are), a small turntable keeps skincare bottles within reach. One spin brings the back row to the front, so nothing gets lost behind taller bottles.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-07-turntable.jpg" alt="Clear turntable on a white cabinet shelf holding skincare bottles, jars and a dropper bottle so items at the back are easy to reach" class="wp-image-1667" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-07-turntable.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-07-turntable-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-07-turntable-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-07-turntable-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">4. Decant Into Matching Pump Bottles</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Matching pump bottles for hand soap or lotion look tidy and are easier to use with one hand. Label each one so nobody mixes them up, and keep the original bottle (or a photo of its label) until you are sure you like the product.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">5. Keep Cotton Rounds and Swabs in Lidded Jars</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Small glass jars with lids keep cotton rounds and swabs clean and dry and make them easy to grab. Two small jars side by side take up less room than an open bag.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">6. Adjust the Glass Shelves</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Most cabinets have adjustable shelves that nobody moves. Before you put anything back, set each shelf to fit what goes on it. A shelf that sits just above your tallest bottle often gives you room for one more row.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Use the Door and Every Inch</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">7. Add a Magnetic Strip Inside the Door</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A slim magnetic strip on the inside of the door holds tweezers, nail clippers and bobby pins where you can see them. Check that the door still closes with the strip and tools in place before you stick it on.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-04-door-magnetic-strip.jpg" alt="Inside of a medicine cabinet door with a magnetic strip holding tweezers, nail clippers and bobby pins and a small cup of makeup brushes" class="wp-image-1664" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-04-door-magnetic-strip.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-04-door-magnetic-strip-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-04-door-magnetic-strip-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-04-door-magnetic-strip-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">8. Stick a Small Cup on the Door</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A small adhesive cup on the door holds makeup brushes or a few cotton swabs and keeps them off the shelf. Pick a shallow one so it does not hit the shelves when the door closes.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">9. Label the Shelves</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Small labels on the shelf edge, like dental, skin and hair, make it easy for everyone in the house to put things back in the right spot. It also helps you notice when you are running low.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Where to Keep Medicines Instead</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">10. Move Pills to a Cool, Dry Drawer</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A lidded box in a bedroom dresser drawer is a simple home for pills, capsules and other medicines. MedlinePlus also advises always keeping medicines in their original containers and taking the cotton ball out of the bottle, since it pulls moisture in. Keep the box out of reach of children, and see our guide on <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/how-to-store-perfume/">how to store perfume</a> for the same cool, dark, dry idea applied to fragrance.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-05-medicines-bedroom-drawer.jpg" alt="Open white oak dresser drawer in a bedroom holding a lidded box of pill bottles, bandages and a small first aid pouch, away from bathroom heat and steam" class="wp-image-1665" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-05-medicines-bedroom-drawer.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-05-medicines-bedroom-drawer-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-05-medicines-bedroom-drawer-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-05-medicines-bedroom-drawer-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">11. Use a Cabinet With a Child Latch</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">MedlinePlus advises always storing medicines in a cabinet with a child latch or lock, which matters most if young children live with or visit you. A high kitchen cabinet away from the stove and sink, with a latch added, works well in many homes.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">12. Give the First Aid Kit One Clear Spot</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Bandages, gauze and tape are easier to find in an emergency when they live in one zippered pouch in a spot everyone knows. A dresser drawer next to your medicines or a hall or <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/linen-closet-organization/">linen closet</a> shelf is a good choice, and you can keep a few bandages in the bathroom cabinet for small scrapes.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Ideas for Small Bathrooms</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">13. Add a Surface-Mounted Cabinet</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">No medicine cabinet at all? A surface-mounted cabinet with a mirror door hangs on the wall like a picture and gives you a mirror and storage in one. For more ways to add storage without a remodel, see our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-bathroom-storage-ideas/">small bathroom storage ideas</a>.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-06-surface-mounted.jpg" alt="Surface-mounted white oak medicine cabinet with a mirror door above a pedestal sink in a small sage green bathroom, door open to show jars and cotton swabs" class="wp-image-1666" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-06-surface-mounted.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-06-surface-mounted-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-06-surface-mounted-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-06-surface-mounted-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">14. Split Storage Between Cabinet, Sink and Toilet</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A small medicine cabinet cannot hold everything. Keep daily items in it, move bulk refills and cleaning products <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/under-bathroom-sink-organization-ideas/">under the sink</a> and use the wall <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/over-the-toilet-storage-ideas/">over the toilet</a> for towels and extra rolls.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">15. Do a Five-Minute Monthly Reset</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Once a month, take out anything that does not belong, wipe the shelves and check dates. It takes five minutes and keeps the cabinet from sliding back into clutter. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/bathroom-organization-ideas/">bathroom organization ideas</a> show how to set the same habit for the whole room.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Get Rid of Old Medicines</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Do not keep expired or unused medicines around. The FDA&#8217;s guide to <a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/safe-disposal-medicines/disposal-unused-medicines-what-you-should-know" target="_blank" rel="noopener">disposing of unused medicines</a> says the best option is a drug take-back location or a prepaid mail-back envelope. If that is not possible and the medicine is not on the FDA flush list, mix it with something unappealing such as dirt, cat litter or used coffee grounds without crushing pills, seal it in a plastic bag and throw it in the trash. Scratch out personal information on the empty bottle before you toss it.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Medicine Cabinet Mistakes to Avoid</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Keeping all your medicines in the bathroom.</strong> Heat and moisture from the shower may damage them.</li>



<li><strong>Holding on to expired products.</strong> Old items take up space and may not work as they should.</li>



<li><strong>Buying bins before measuring.</strong> Many cabinets are shallow, and a bin that is too deep stops the door from closing.</li>



<li><strong>Overfilling the shelves.</strong> If things fall out when you open the door, remove a few items.</li>



<li><strong>Leaving medicines within reach of children.</strong> Use a high cabinet with a latch or a lock box.</li>
</ul>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-08-before-after.jpg" alt="Medicine cabinet before and after: crowded shelves with toppled bottles, hair ties and loose swabs, then the same cabinet sorted into clear bins with space on every shelf" class="wp-image-1668" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-08-before-after.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-08-before-after-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-08-before-after-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/medicine-cabinet-08-before-after-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What should be kept in a medicine cabinet?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Daily toiletries you use at the sink: toothpaste, floss, mouthwash, skincare, deodorant, cotton rounds, swabs and small grooming tools. Most medicines are better kept in a cool, dry place outside the bathroom.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Is it bad to keep medicine in the bathroom?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It is not ideal. MedlinePlus notes that heat and moisture from the shower, bath and sink may damage medicines and suggests a dresser drawer or a kitchen cabinet away from the stove, sink and hot appliances.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How do you organize a small medicine cabinet?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Adjust the shelves to fit your bottles, keep daily items at eye level, group small things in narrow clear bins, use the inside of the door for tools and move medicines and bulk refills elsewhere.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How do you get rid of expired medicine?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Use a drug take-back location or a mail-back envelope if you can. Otherwise, follow the FDA&#8217;s household trash steps: mix the medicine with something unappealing, seal it in a bag and throw it away, unless it is on the FDA flush list.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Medicine cabinet organization is mostly about deciding what belongs there. Keep the cabinet for daily toiletries, sort them by how often you use them, contain small things in clear bins and move your medicines to a cool, dry place out of children&#8217;s reach. Then a monthly five-minute reset keeps it that way.</p>



